      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "act": "THE EXAMPLE",
      "estimated_duration_s": 14.0,
      "narration_text": "Take an illustrative scenario. A nanoparticle therapy shrinks a mouse tumor rim by sixty percent over three weeks. At week three, IFP at the core measures twenty-five millimeters of mercury. At the rim: five. The core — viable, two millimeters across — sits behind a pressure wall the particles cannot cross. By week eight, tumor volume has doubled. The growth originates from the core.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "drawon"
      },
      "scene_class": "B10_Example",
      "audio_file": "../vox-tumor-pressure/mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 20.16,
      "locked": true,
      "source_clip": "../vox-tumor-pressure/clips/B10.mp4",
      "source_audio": "../vox-tumor-pressure/mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B10.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-08-30T20:22:24"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "act": "RECAP",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11.0,
      "narration_text": "The drug reached the tumor. The leaky vessels that let it in also flooded the interstitium and drove fluid outward. Particles stayed at the rim. The core survived, selected for resistance, and grew back. The accumulation was real. The delivery was not.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
